window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"AWS SAA Field Guide","section":"Architect on AWS. Pass the exam.","tool":"home","text":"Architect on AWS. Pass the exam. A sharp, visual path from zero AWS to Solutions Architect – Associate (SAA-C03) — foundations, networking, identity, compute, storage, data, integration, resilience, and cost. Every lesson ends with exam-style rapid-fire. As of September 2026.","keywords":["architect","on","aws","pass","the","exam"]},{"url":"index.html#progress","page":"AWS SAA Field Guide","section":"Your progress","tool":"home","text":"01 Your progress Mark lessons \"learned\" as you go — progress stays in this browser. Resume anywhere or jump into a module below. Start learning Reset progress","keywords":["your","progress"]},{"url":"index.html#path","page":"AWS SAA Field Guide","section":"The journey, zero to exam","tool":"home","text":"02 The journey, zero to exam Build the mental model first, then design decisions mapped to the four SAA domains: Secure · Resilient · High-performing · Cost-optimized. Start · get oriented Foundations Regions & AZs, shared responsibility, Well-Architected, and how SAA-C03 stems are written. Cloud mo","keywords":["the","journey","zero","to","exam"]},{"url":"index.html#modules","page":"AWS SAA Field Guide","section":"Explore the modules","tool":"home","text":"03 Explore the modules Eight learning modules plus exam prep and reference. Start at Foundations if you're new to AWS. Start Foundations Cloud mental model, Regions/AZs, Well-Architected, shared responsibility, exam method. Start -> Connect Networking VPC, SG/NACL, endpoints, Route 53, CloudFront. O","keywords":["explore","the","modules"]},{"url":"foundations/cloud-mental-model.html","page":"Cloud mental model","section":"Think in building blocks, not boxes.","tool":"foundations","text":"Think in building blocks, not boxes. SAA-C03 rewards architects who map requirements to AWS primitives — compute, network, data, identity — and pick the managed service that fits durability, scale, and blast radius. Skip “which logo?”; decide “what failure mode and who owns the patching?”","keywords":["think","in","building","blocks","not","boxes"]},{"url":"foundations/cloud-mental-model.html#blocks","page":"Cloud mental model","section":"The five blocks every design uses","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The five blocks every design uses Every SAA answer is a composition of compute , network , storage/data , identity , and edge/integration . Name the block first; the service second. In plain English analogy AWS is a city of prefabricated rooms, not a blank lot. You don’t pour concrete for a kitch","keywords":["the","five","blocks","every","design","uses"]},{"url":"foundations/cloud-mental-model.html#managed","page":"Cloud mental model","section":"Managed vs you-operate","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Managed vs you-operate Default to managed when the exam cites undifferentiated ops (patching, Multi-AZ failover, backups). Pick self-managed only when you need OS/kernel control or a constraint the managed service can’t meet. You operate EC2 + self-installed DB You own patches, HA, backups Exam u","keywords":["managed","vs","you","operate"]},{"url":"foundations/cloud-mental-model.html#exam-lens","page":"Cloud mental model","section":"The exam decision lens","tool":"foundations","text":"03 The exam decision lens Read stems for constraints : latency, RPO/RTO, compliance, cost, throughput, “no internet,” “least privilege.” Constraints kill otherwise-valid options. 1 Underline the hard requirement Words like must , cannot leave the VPC , global users , cost-effective , strong consiste","keywords":["the","exam","decision","lens"]},{"url":"foundations/cloud-mental-model.html#interview","page":"Cloud mental model","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire How do you approach a vague “design a web app on AWS” stem? warm-up Model answer I name the blocks: public entry (ALB or CloudFront+ALB), compute in private subnets (ASG or Fargate), data store Multi-AZ (RDS/Aurora or Dynamo), IAM roles not keys, and decide Multi-AZ vs Multi-Regio","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/regions-azs-edge.html","page":"Regions, AZs & the edge","section":"Region, AZ, edge — pick the blast radius.","tool":"foundations","text":"Region, AZ, edge — pick the blast radius. Availability Zones are the unit of high availability inside a Region. Regions are the unit of disaster recovery and data residency. Edge locations shrink latency for users far from your Region — they don’t replace Multi-AZ for your stateful tier.","keywords":["region","az","edge","pick","the","blast","radius"]},{"url":"foundations/regions-azs-edge.html#hierarchy","page":"Regions, AZs & the edge","section":"The location hierarchy","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The location hierarchy A Region is a geographic area with multiple isolated Availability Zones (data centers with independent power/networking). Edge locations / Points of Presence sit closer to users for CDN and DNS. In plain English analogy A Region is a city. Each AZ is a separate power grid a","keywords":["the","location","hierarchy"]},{"url":"foundations/regions-azs-edge.html#ha-vs-dr","page":"Regions, AZs & the edge","section":"Multi-AZ vs multi-Region","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Multi-AZ vs multi-Region Multi-AZ survives an AZ failure (typical HA). Multi-Region survives a Region event and meets residency/latency needs — higher cost and complexity. Goal Pattern Exam signal Survive one AZ outage Multi-AZ ASG, ALB, RDS Multi-AZ, Aurora “highly available within a Region” Sur","keywords":["multi","az","vs","region"]},{"url":"foundations/regions-azs-edge.html#defaults","page":"Regions, AZs & the edge","section":"Modern defaults (2026)","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Modern defaults (2026) For stateful data in one Region: Multi-AZ by default . For static/global content: S3 + CloudFront . For DNS failover: Route 53 health checks only when you truly have a secondary. Under-scoped Single-AZ RDS “to save money” ASG min=2, all in us-east-1a Assuming edge = Region ","keywords":["modern","defaults"]},{"url":"foundations/regions-azs-edge.html#interview","page":"Regions, AZs & the edge","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Why can’t a subnet span two AZs? warm-up Model answer A subnet is bound to exactly one AZ. HA means creating subnets in each AZ and placing resources (or letting ASG/RDS place them) across those subnets. Say it out loud say it \"One subnet, one AZ — so for HA I create matching subn","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/well-architected.html","page":"Well-Architected Framework","section":"Six pillars, one design conversation.","tool":"foundations","text":"Six pillars, one design conversation. The AWS Well-Architected Framework is how SAA-C03 grades trade-offs: operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization, and sustainability. Use pillars to eliminate answers that ignore a stated constraint.","keywords":["six","pillars","one","design","conversation"]},{"url":"foundations/well-architected.html#pillars","page":"Well-Architected Framework","section":"The six pillars (exam map)","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The six pillars (exam map) Each SAA domain echoes WA pillars. When stuck between two “correct” services, ask which pillar the stem emphasizes. In plain English analogy Pillars are like building codes for a skyscraper: fire safety (security), earthquake bracing (reliability), elevator capacity (pe","keywords":["the","six","pillars","exam","map"]},{"url":"foundations/well-architected.html#tradeoffs","page":"Well-Architected Framework","section":"Pillars collide — pick intentionally","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Pillars collide — pick intentionally There is no free lunch: Multi-Region reliability costs money; aggressive caching can stale data; tight security can add latency. The stem tells you which pillar wins. Stem says “cost-effective” Prefer Single-Region Multi-AZ over active-active Gateway endpoints","keywords":["pillars","collide","pick","intentionally"]},{"url":"foundations/well-architected.html#review","page":"Well-Architected Framework","section":"Lightweight review habit","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Lightweight review habit Before locking an answer, run a 20-second WA checklist: identity? blast radius? failover path? right-sized? idle cost? 1 Security No public DB; roles not keys; encrypt; least privilege SG. 2 Reliability ≥2 AZs; health checks; backups match RPO. 3 Perf & cost Cache/edge if","keywords":["lightweight","review","habit"]},{"url":"foundations/well-architected.html#interview","page":"Well-Architected Framework","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Name the six Well-Architected pillars. warm-up Model answer Operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization, and sustainability. Say it out loud say it \"Ops, security, reliability, performance, cost, and sustainability — six lenses on every","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/shared-responsibility.html","page":"Shared responsibility","section":"AWS secures the cloud; you secure in it.","tool":"foundations","text":"AWS secures the cloud; you secure in it. Shared Responsibility is the security framing behind IAM, encryption, patching, and network isolation questions. The line moves with the service model — more managed means AWS owns more of the stack, but never your data classification or identity choices.","keywords":["aws","secures","the","cloud","you","secure","in","it"]},{"url":"foundations/shared-responsibility.html#line","page":"Shared responsibility","section":"Where the line sits","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Where the line sits AWS is responsible for security of the cloud (facilities, hardware, virtualization, global network). You are responsible for security in the cloud (OS/apps where applicable, data, identity, network config, encryption choices). In plain English analogy AWS is the landlord of a ","keywords":["where","the","line","sits"]},{"url":"foundations/shared-responsibility.html#matrix","page":"Shared responsibility","section":"Exam matrix: who does what","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Exam matrix: who does what Memorize a few sharp splits — they appear as “who is responsible for…” distractors. Concern EC2 RDS Lambda / S3 Hypervisor / facilities AWS AWS AWS Guest OS patching Customer AWS AWS Network controls (VPC/SG) Customer Customer Customer (config) Data classification & enc","keywords":["exam","matrix","who","does","what"]},{"url":"foundations/shared-responsibility.html#modern","page":"Shared responsibility","section":"Modern identity defaults","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Modern identity defaults Prefer IAM roles and IAM Identity Center over long-lived access keys. Prefer instance/task/Lambda roles and OIDC federation from CI. Humans Identity Center (SSO) → short-lived roles into accounts; MFA enforced. Workloads EC2 instance profiles, ECS task roles, Lambda execu","keywords":["modern","identity","defaults","yum","update"]},{"url":"foundations/shared-responsibility.html#interview","page":"Shared responsibility","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire State shared responsibility in one sentence. warm-up Model answer AWS secures the cloud infrastructure; customers secure what they put in it — identities, configurations, applications, and data — with the customer share shrinking as the service becomes more managed. Say it out lou","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html","page":"How to take SAA-C03","section":"How to take SAA-C03 without drowning.","tool":"foundations","text":"How to take SAA-C03 without drowning. SAA-C03 is a timed scenario exam: eliminate wrong architectures, don’t invent services. As of 2026 the four domains still drive scoring — Secure, Resilient, High-performing, Cost-optimized. Method beats memorizing every feature flag.","keywords":["how","to","take","saa","c03","without","drowning"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#domains","page":"How to take SAA-C03","section":"Know what you’re graded on","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Know what you’re graded on Every question maps to at least one domain. Train your eye to spot domain keywords before you fall in love with a service name. In plain English analogy Think of the exam as four different judges scoring the same blueprint. One cares about locks, one about earthquakes, ","keywords":["know","what","you","re","graded","on"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#technique","page":"How to take SAA-C03","section":"Elimination technique","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Elimination technique Most stems have one hard constraint that kills 2–3 options. Circle it. Then pick the simplest remaining AWS-native design. 1 Underline constraints Must , cannot , RPO/RTO, “private only,” “global users,” “least operational overhead.” 2 Kill anti-patterns Public RDS, long-liv","keywords":["elimination","technique"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#study","page":"How to take SAA-C03","section":"What to memorize vs reason","tool":"foundations","text":"03 What to memorize vs reason Memorize sharp dichotomies (SG vs NACL, gateway vs interface endpoint, ALB vs NLB). Reason about scenarios using Well-Architected trade-offs. Memorize SG stateful allow-only vs NACL stateless S3/DynamoDB gateway endpoints are free of hourly ENI charges RDS Multi-AZ = sy","keywords":["what","to","memorize","vs","reason"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#interview","page":"How to take SAA-C03","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire What’s your first 10 seconds on a hard SAA question? warm-up Model answer Find the hard constraint and the domain keyword. Mentally discard anything that violates them before comparing the remaining two options on cost or ops overhead. Say it out loud say it \"Constraint first, eli","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"networking/vpc-and-subnets.html","page":"VPC & subnets","section":"Your private network on AWS.","tool":"networking","text":"Your private network on AWS. A VPC is an isolated IPv4/IPv6 network you control. Subnets carve it per AZ; public vs private is just routing — not a magic flag. SAA designs put load balancers (and NAT) public, apps and data private, across ≥2 AZs.","keywords":["your","private","network","on","aws"]},{"url":"networking/vpc-and-subnets.html#vpc-basics","page":"VPC & subnets","section":"CIDR, subnets, and AZ pinning","tool":"networking","text":"01 CIDR, subnets, and AZ pinning Pick a VPC CIDR (commonly 10.0.0.0/16 ), slice non-overlapping subnet CIDRs, and place each subnet in exactly one AZ . Plan headroom for growth and secondary CIDRs if needed. In plain English analogy The VPC is a gated campus. Each building (subnet) sits on one city ","keywords":["cidr","subnets","and","az","pinning"]},{"url":"networking/vpc-and-subnets.html#request-path","page":"VPC & subnets","section":"Canonical multi-AZ request path","tool":"networking","text":"02 Canonical multi-AZ request path Internet → IGW → ALB in public subnets (2+ AZs) → targets in private subnets → RDS Multi-AZ privately. Outbound from private tiers via NAT or VPC endpoints. Inbound HTTPS through a multi-AZ VPC ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× Internet → IGW → ALB in public multi-AZ subnet","keywords":["canonical","multi","az","request","path"]},{"url":"networking/vpc-and-subnets.html#public-private","page":"VPC & subnets","section":"Public vs private — exam rules","tool":"networking","text":"03 Public vs private — exam rules Public subnet: 0.0.0.0/0 → IGW (+ usually auto-assign public IP for instances that need it). Private: default route to NAT or no internet — never place RDS in public for convenience. Anti-pattern RDS / ElastiCache in public subnets App servers with public IPs “so SS","keywords":["public","vs","private","exam","rules"]},{"url":"networking/vpc-and-subnets.html#interview","page":"VPC & subnets","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"networking","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire What makes a subnet “public”? warm-up Model answer Its route table has a default route to an Internet Gateway. The name isn’t a checkbox — routing defines it. Instances usually also need a public or Elastic IP to be reachable inbound. Say it out loud say it \"Public means the subne","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"networking/routing-sg-nacl.html","page":"Routing, security groups & NACLs","section":"Routes decide reachability; firewalls decide permission.","tool":"networking","text":"Routes decide reachability; firewalls decide permission. Route tables answer “where does this packet go?” Security groups and NACLs answer “is it allowed?” Master stateful vs stateless — it’s one of the highest-frequency SAA networking discriminators.","keywords":["routes","decide","reachability","firewalls","permission"]},{"url":"networking/routing-sg-nacl.html#routes","page":"Routing, security groups & NACLs","section":"Route tables that matter","tool":"networking","text":"01 Route tables that matter Each subnet associates with one route table. Longest-prefix match wins. Default local route keeps VPC-internal traffic private; you add IGW, NAT, endpoints, peering, TGW, or VPN targets. In plain English analogy The route table is the campus map: “to leave campus, use Gat","keywords":["route","tables","that","matter"]},{"url":"networking/routing-sg-nacl.html#sg-nacl","page":"Routing, security groups & NACLs","section":"Security groups vs NACLs","tool":"networking","text":"02 Security groups vs NACLs Security groups attach to ENIs: stateful, allow-only, evaluated as a set. NACLs attach to subnets: stateless, numbered allow/deny, first match wins. Security group NACL Scope ENI / instance / LB / RDS Subnet State Stateful Stateless Rules Allow only Allow and deny Default","keywords":["security","groups","vs","nacls"]},{"url":"networking/routing-sg-nacl.html#patterns","page":"Routing, security groups & NACLs","section":"Tier patterns that pass the exam","tool":"networking","text":"03 Tier patterns that pass the exam ALB SG allows 443 from the world (or CloudFront prefix). App SG allows only from ALB SG. DB SG allows only from app SG. NACLs stay permissive unless you need an explicit deny. Loose App SG: inbound 0.0.0.0/0 on 8080 DB SG: inbound from whole VPC CIDR Rely on NACL ","keywords":["tier","patterns","that","pass","the","exam","sg","alb","app"]},{"url":"networking/routing-sg-nacl.html#interview","page":"Routing, security groups & NACLs","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"networking","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Stateful vs stateless — say it cleanly. warm-up Model answer Security groups are stateful: allow outbound and the response is automatically allowed. NACLs are stateless: every direction needs an explicit rule. Say it out loud say it \"SG remembers the conversation; NACL makes you a","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"networking/endpoints-privatelink.html","page":"VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","section":"Stay private to AWS — and cut the NAT bill.","tool":"networking","text":"Stay private to AWS — and cut the NAT bill. VPC endpoints keep traffic to AWS services on the Amazon network without IGW/NAT. Gateway endpoints cover S3 and DynamoDB; interface endpoints (PrivateLink) bring many APIs into your subnets as ENIs. PrivateLink also exposes your own services to other VPCs","keywords":["stay","private","to","aws","and","cut","the","nat","bill"]},{"url":"networking/endpoints-privatelink.html#why","page":"VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","section":"Why endpoints show up constantly","tool":"networking","text":"01 Why endpoints show up constantly Exam stems that say no internet , private only , or reduce data transfer/NAT cost almost always want VPC endpoints — not “add a NAT and open 443 everywhere.” In plain English analogy Without endpoints, private apps leave campus through the highway tollbooth (NAT) ","keywords":["why","endpoints","show","up","constantly"]},{"url":"networking/endpoints-privatelink.html#gateway-vs-interface","page":"VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","section":"Gateway vs interface endpoints","tool":"networking","text":"02 Gateway vs interface endpoints Gateway endpoints: S3 & DynamoDB only — route-table targets, no ENI hourly charge. Interface endpoints: PrivateLink ENIs in your subnets — security groups, DNS private names, hourly + data processing charges. Gateway endpoint Interface endpoint (PrivateLink) Service","keywords":["gateway","vs","interface","endpoints"]},{"url":"networking/endpoints-privatelink.html#privatelink","page":"VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","section":"PrivateLink for your services","tool":"networking","text":"03 PrivateLink for your services Expose an NLB-fronted service via an endpoint service ; consumers create interface endpoints in their VPCs. No overlapping CIDR fights, no transitive peering mess — ideal for SaaS-to-customer private connectivity. Choose PrivateLink when Many consumer VPCs need one p","keywords":["privatelink","for","your","services"]},{"url":"networking/endpoints-privatelink.html#interview","page":"VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"networking","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Which services get gateway endpoints? warm-up Model answer Amazon S3 and DynamoDB. Everything else that supports PrivateLink uses interface endpoints. Say it out loud say it \"Gateway endpoints: S3 and DynamoDB. Other AWS APIs use interface PrivateLink endpoints.\" PrivateLink vs VP","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"networking/route53-dns.html","page":"Route 53 & DNS patterns","section":"DNS that steers traffic — and fails over.","tool":"networking","text":"DNS that steers traffic — and fails over. Route 53 is authoritative DNS plus health-aware routing. SAA cares less about registrar trivia and more about record types, routing policies, alias vs CNAME, and private hosted zones for VPC name resolution.","keywords":["dns","that","steers","traffic","and","fails","over"]},{"url":"networking/route53-dns.html#basics","page":"Route 53 & DNS patterns","section":"Public vs private hosted zones","tool":"networking","text":"01 Public vs private hosted zones Public hosted zones answer on the internet. Private hosted zones answer inside associated VPCs — split-horizon friendly for internal service names. In plain English analogy Public DNS is the phone book everyone can buy. A private hosted zone is the internal company ","keywords":["public","vs","private","hosted","zones","example","com"]},{"url":"networking/route53-dns.html#policies","page":"Route 53 & DNS patterns","section":"Routing policies that matter on SAA","tool":"networking","text":"02 Routing policies that matter on SAA Pick the policy from the stem’s goal: simple, failover, latency, geolocation/geoproximity, weighted, multivalue. flowchart TD q[DNS_query] --> pol{Routing_policy} pol -->|simple| one[Single_record] pol -->|failover| primary[Primary_or_secondary] pol -->|latency","keywords":["routing","policies","that","matter","on","saa"]},{"url":"networking/route53-dns.html#health","page":"Route 53 & DNS patterns","section":"Health checks & modern pairings","tool":"networking","text":"03 Health checks & modern pairings Route 53 health checks probe endpoints (or calculated/CloudWatch alarms). Pair with failover or multivalue. For global anycast acceleration of TCP/UDP, consider Global Accelerator — DNS still often points there. Route 53 latency / failover Works at DNS decision tim","keywords":["health","checks","modern","pairings","example","com"]},{"url":"networking/route53-dns.html#interview","page":"Route 53 & DNS patterns","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"networking","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Alias vs CNAME — when must you use alias? warm-up Model answer For the zone apex and when pointing to AWS resources like ALB/CloudFront/S3 website endpoints. Alias is Route 53-specific and avoids apex CNAME limitations. Say it out loud say it \"Apex to ALB or CloudFront needs a Rou","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire","db","internal"]},{"url":"networking/cloudfront-global.html","page":"CloudFront & global edge","section":"Put the edge in front of your origin.","tool":"networking","text":"Put the edge in front of your origin. CloudFront is AWS’s CDN: cache static (and some dynamic) content at edge locations, terminate TLS close to users, and shield origins. Pair with S3 (OAC), ALB, or custom origins — and remember edge performance ≠ origin Multi-AZ.","keywords":["put","the","edge","in","front","of","your","origin"]},{"url":"networking/cloudfront-global.html#model","page":"CloudFront & global edge","section":"How CloudFront fits the request path","tool":"networking","text":"01 How CloudFront fits the request path Viewer → edge (cache hit or miss) → optional regional edge cache → origin . Cache behaviors map path patterns to origins, TTLs, and viewer protocols. In plain English analogy CloudFront is a chain of neighborhood libraries. Popular books (static assets) sit on","keywords":["how","cloudfront","fits","the","request","path"]},{"url":"networking/cloudfront-global.html#s3-oac","page":"CloudFront & global edge","section":"S3 origins the modern way","tool":"networking","text":"02 S3 origins the modern way Use Origin Access Control (OAC) so the bucket stays private and only CloudFront can read it. Prefer OAC over legacy OAI. Don’t make the bucket public “for the CDN.” Anti-pattern Public S3 website endpoint as origin Bucket policy allows * Assuming CloudFront encrypts obje","keywords":["s3","origins","the","modern","way","CachingDisabled"]},{"url":"networking/cloudfront-global.html#extras","page":"CloudFront & global edge","section":"Performance, security, and failover extras","tool":"networking","text":"03 Performance, security, and failover extras CloudFront + WAF for L7 filters; Shield Standard included, Shield Advanced optional. Origin groups give primary/secondary origin failover. Lambda@Edge / CloudFront Functions for lightweight request surgery at the edge. 1 Static global S3 + CloudFront + O","keywords":["performance","security","and","failover","extras","static"]},{"url":"networking/cloudfront-global.html#interview","page":"CloudFront & global edge","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"networking","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Why put CloudFront in front of S3? warm-up Model answer Lower latency via edge caching, lower S3 request/data cost for hot objects, HTTPS at the edge, and the ability to keep the bucket private with OAC while still serving the world. Say it out loud say it \"CloudFront caches at th","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"identity-security/iam-users-roles-policies.html","page":"IAM users, roles & policies","section":"IAM users, roles & policies.","tool":"identity-security","text":"IAM users, roles & policies. Who can call which API on which resource. SAA-C03 defaults (Sep 2026): roles + short-lived credentials , not long-lived access keys; humans via Identity Center; workloads via instance profiles, IRSA/Pod Identity, or OIDC.","keywords":["iam","users","roles","policies"]},{"url":"identity-security/iam-users-roles-policies.html#identities","page":"IAM users, roles & policies","section":"Users, groups, roles — pick the right principal","tool":"identity-security","text":"01 Users, groups, roles — pick the right principal An IAM user is a long-lived principal (password and/or access keys). A role is assumed and yields temporary credentials. A group is only a permission bundle for users — never attach policies to “everyone” via wildcard principals. In plain English an","keywords":["users","groups","roles","pick","the","right","principal","Action","Resource"]},{"url":"identity-security/iam-users-roles-policies.html#policies","page":"IAM users, roles & policies","section":"Policies: identity, resource & permission boundaries","tool":"identity-security","text":"02 Policies: identity, resource & permission boundaries Permissions are JSON. Evaluation is not “any Allow wins” — explicit Deny always wins , then Allow must grant the action on the resource, and SCPs / boundaries can still block. flowchart TD request[API_request] --> deny{Explicit_Deny?} deny -->|","keywords":["policies","identity","resource","permission","boundaries","Version","Statement","Sid","ReadOneBucket","Effect","Allow","Action","s3","GetObject","Resource","arn","aws","acme","invoices","Condition","StringEquals","RequestedRegion","us","east","SourceVpce"]},{"url":"identity-security/iam-users-roles-policies.html#workload-creds","page":"IAM users, roles & policies","section":"Workload credentials without keys","tool":"identity-security","text":"03 Workload credentials without keys EC2 Instance profile → role; SDK pulls rotating creds from IMDS. Prefer IMDSv2. Lambda / ECS Execution / task role attached at deploy time — no keys in env. EKS IRSA or EKS Pod Identity maps a service account to an IAM role. See containers & serverless . CI/CD OI","keywords":["workload","credentials","without","keys","AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity","AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY","s3","GetObject","etc","environment"]},{"url":"identity-security/iam-users-roles-policies.html#interview","page":"IAM users, roles & policies","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"identity-security","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire User vs role — when is each correct? core Model answer Prefer roles for anything that needs AWS API access: workloads, federation, cross-account. IAM users with long-lived keys are legacy for apps; humans should use Identity Center. Roles issue temporary STS credentials that ","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"identity-security/encryption-kms-secrets.html","page":"Encryption, KMS & secrets","section":"Encryption, KMS & secrets.","tool":"identity-security","text":"Encryption, KMS & secrets. Encrypt data at rest and in transit; keep keys in KMS ; keep credentials in Secrets Manager or SSM — never in code. Exam scenarios mix SSE-S3 vs SSE-KMS, CMK key policies, and rotation.","keywords":["encryption","kms","secrets"]},{"url":"identity-security/encryption-kms-secrets.html#kms-model","page":"Encryption, KMS & secrets","section":"KMS mental model","tool":"identity-security","text":"01 KMS mental model KMS manages cryptographic keys. You rarely encrypt big blobs with KMS directly — services use envelope encryption : a data key encrypts the object; KMS encrypts the data key. In plain English analogy Think of a locked filing cabinet (your S3 object) and a small key in a sealed en","keywords":["kms","mental","model","aws","s3","ebs"]},{"url":"identity-security/encryption-kms-secrets.html#at-rest","page":"Encryption, KMS & secrets","section":"Encryption at rest by service","tool":"identity-security","text":"02 Encryption at rest by service Service Default / common Exam cue S3 SSE-S3 (AES-256) or SSE-KMS; DSSE-KMS for dual-layer Need audit of who decrypted → SSE-KMS + CloudTrail EBS Encrypt volumes/snapshots with KMS Encrypted snapshots stay encrypted when shared carefully RDS / Aurora Enable encryption","keywords":["encryption","at","rest","by","service","kms","Decrypt","GenerateDataKey"]},{"url":"identity-security/encryption-kms-secrets.html#secrets","page":"Encryption, KMS & secrets","section":"Secrets Manager vs Parameter Store","tool":"identity-security","text":"03 Secrets Manager vs Parameter Store Secrets Manager Built for credentials & API keys Automatic rotation (Lambda rotators, RDS integration) Cross-account sharing patterns Higher per-secret cost SSM Parameter Store Config + simple secrets ( SecureString + KMS) Hierarchical paths, cheap standard para","keywords":["secrets","manager","vs","parameter","store","SecureString"]},{"url":"identity-security/encryption-kms-secrets.html#interview","page":"Encryption, KMS & secrets","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"identity-security","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire SSE-S3 vs SSE-KMS — when do you pick KMS? core Model answer SSE-S3 encrypts with AWS-managed S3 keys — simple and fine for many workloads. Choose SSE-KMS when you need a customer-managed key policy, separate IAM grants for decrypt, CloudTrail visibility into key use, or cross","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","SecureString"]},{"url":"identity-security/org-scp-identity-center.html","page":"Organizations, SCPs & Identity Center","section":"Organizations, SCPs & Identity Center.","tool":"identity-security","text":"Organizations, SCPs & Identity Center. Multi-account is the Sep 2026 default: isolate prod, apply SCPs as guardrails, and sign humans in with IAM Identity Center (SSO) — not shared IAM users or long-lived keys.","keywords":["organizations","scps","identity","center"]},{"url":"identity-security/org-scp-identity-center.html#orgs","page":"Organizations, SCPs & Identity Center","section":"Organizations & account strategy","tool":"identity-security","text":"01 Organizations & account strategy AWS Organizations groups accounts under one bill and one policy tree. Typical OUs: Security, Infrastructure, Sandbox, Workloads (dev/test/prod). One app blast radius ≠ one huge account. In plain English analogy Think of a company campus: each building (account) ha","keywords":["organizations","account","strategy"]},{"url":"identity-security/org-scp-identity-center.html#scps","page":"Organizations, SCPs & Identity Center","section":"SCPs: guardrails, not grants","tool":"identity-security","text":"02 SCPs: guardrails, not grants SCPs never grant permissions — they set the maximum available in an account. IAM still must Allow. FullAWSAccess is the default; tighten with Deny statements (regions, leave-org, disable CloudTrail, root actions). deny-unapproved-regions.json Copy { \"Version\": \"2012-1","keywords":["scps","guardrails","not","grants","Version","Statement","Sid","DenyOutsideEU","Effect","Deny","NotAction","cloudfront","iam","organizations","route53","support","Resource","Condition","StringNotEquals","aws","RequestedRegion","eu","west","central"]},{"url":"identity-security/org-scp-identity-center.html#identity-center","page":"Organizations, SCPs & Identity Center","section":"IAM Identity Center (SSO)","tool":"identity-security","text":"03 IAM Identity Center (SSO) Identity Center is how humans get into AWS in 2026: workforce identity → permission sets → accounts. Temporary credentials via AWS access portal; optional ABAC with attributes. Identity Center One portal for many accounts Permission sets ≈ managed role templates Integrat","keywords":["iam","identity","center","sso"]},{"url":"identity-security/org-scp-identity-center.html#interview","page":"Organizations, SCPs & Identity Center","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"identity-security","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What do SCPs actually do? core Model answer SCPs are organization guardrails that define the ceiling of permissions in member accounts. They don’t grant anything by themselves — IAM identity and resource policies still must Allow. Use them to deny dangerous actions or unappro","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"identity-security/edge-waf-shield.html","page":"WAF, Shield & edge protection","section":"WAF, Shield & edge protection.","tool":"identity-security","text":"WAF, Shield & edge protection. Stop volumetric and application-layer attacks before they hit your origin. Pair CloudFront / ALB / API Gateway with AWS WAF , and know when Shield Standard vs Advanced matters.","keywords":["waf","shield","edge","protection"]},{"url":"identity-security/edge-waf-shield.html#layers","page":"WAF, Shield & edge protection","section":"Edge protection stack","tool":"identity-security","text":"01 Edge protection stack Defense in depth at the edge: DNS (Route 53) → CDN (CloudFront) → WAF rules → Shield DDoS → origin (ALB/S3/custom) locked to the edge. In plain English analogy Shield is the riot police for floods of traffic (DDoS). WAF is the bouncer reading IDs and bag checks — SQL injecti","keywords":["edge","protection","stack"]},{"url":"identity-security/edge-waf-shield.html#waf","page":"WAF, Shield & edge protection","section":"AWS WAF essentials","tool":"identity-security","text":"02 AWS WAF essentials Web ACL Collection of rules attached to CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway, or AppSync. Managed rule groups AWS / marketplace packs for SQLi, XSS, known bad inputs — start here on the exam. Rate-based rules Throttle abusive IPs (login scraping, credential stuffing). Geo / IP sets All","keywords":["aws","waf","essentials"]},{"url":"identity-security/edge-waf-shield.html#shield","page":"WAF, Shield & edge protection","section":"Shield Standard vs Advanced","tool":"identity-security","text":"03 Shield Standard vs Advanced Shield Standard Shield Advanced Cost Included with AWS Subscription + data transfer Coverage Common L3/L4 network DDoS on many services Enhanced detection, tighter mitigations, cost protection, SRT access Integrations Baseline WAF credits, Route 53 Application Recovery","keywords":["shield","standard","vs","advanced"]},{"url":"identity-security/edge-waf-shield.html#interview","page":"WAF, Shield & edge protection","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"identity-security","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire WAF vs Shield — one sentence each? core Model answer WAF filters HTTP(S) requests with rules (SQLi, XSS, rate, geo). Shield mitigates DDoS at the network/transport layers (and Advanced adds operational support and extra protections). Often used together in front of CloudFront","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"identity-security/detective-controls.html","page":"Detective controls","section":"Detective controls.","tool":"identity-security","text":"Detective controls. Preventive IAM is not enough. SAA expects you to wire CloudTrail , Config , GuardDuty , Security Hub , and CloudWatch so misconfigs and threats surface fast — centrally in multi-account orgs.","keywords":["detective","controls"]},{"url":"identity-security/detective-controls.html#trail-config","page":"Detective controls","section":"CloudTrail & Config","tool":"identity-security","text":"01 CloudTrail & Config CloudTrail answers “who did what, when?” Config answers “what does my inventory look like, and is it compliant?” In plain English analogy CloudTrail is the building’s CCTV + badge log — every door open is recorded. Config is the nightly inventory clipboard : “are all fire exti","keywords":["cloudtrail","config"]},{"url":"identity-security/detective-controls.html#threat","page":"Detective controls","section":"GuardDuty, Security Hub & Inspector","tool":"identity-security","text":"02 GuardDuty, Security Hub & Inspector Service Detects Exam cue GuardDuty Suspicious API use, crypto mining, C2, unusual geolocation Enable org-wide; findings to EventBridge Security Hub Aggregates findings + CIS / FSBP standards “Single pane” multi-account posture Inspector CVE / network reachabili","keywords":["guardduty","security","hub","inspector"]},{"url":"identity-security/detective-controls.html#ops-signals","page":"Detective controls","section":"CloudWatch as a detective signal","tool":"identity-security","text":"03 CloudWatch as a detective signal Metrics & alarms 5xx on ALB, ASG InService count, Lambda errors/throttles. Logs Insights Query app and VPC Flow Logs when GuardDuty points at an ENI. Metric filters Alert on CloudTrail events: root login, auth failures, SG changes. PrivateLink mindset Prefer inter","keywords":["cloudwatch","as","detective","signal"]},{"url":"identity-security/detective-controls.html#interview","page":"Detective controls","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"identity-security","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire CloudTrail vs Config vs GuardDuty? core Model answer CloudTrail records API activity. Config records resource configuration and evaluates rules. GuardDuty analyzes CloudTrail, VPC Flow, and DNS logs for threat findings. Use all three in a mature account baseline. Say it out l","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"compute/ec2-asg-placement.html","page":"EC2, ASG & placement","section":"EC2, ASG & placement.","tool":"compute","text":"EC2, ASG & placement. VMs when you need OS control. Sep 2026 defaults: Graviton where software allows, Multi-AZ ASGs, IMDSv2, instance profiles (no keys), and the right purchase model for steady vs spiky load.","keywords":["ec2","asg","placement"]},{"url":"compute/ec2-asg-placement.html#ec2-basics","page":"EC2, ASG & placement","section":"Instance shape & Graviton","tool":"compute","text":"01 Instance shape & Graviton Pick family for the bottleneck (compute, memory, storage, GPU, network), then generation. Prefer Arm/Graviton (e.g. m7g, c7g) for better price-performance when binaries support it. In plain English analogy EC2 is renting a computer in someone else’s datacenter. An ASG is","keywords":["instance","shape","graviton"]},{"url":"compute/ec2-asg-placement.html#asg","page":"EC2, ASG & placement","section":"Auto Scaling groups","tool":"compute","text":"02 Auto Scaling groups ASG maintains desired capacity across AZs, replaces unhealthy instances (ELB/EC2 health checks), and scales on metrics or schedules/predictive policies. flowchart TD users[Users] --> alb[ALB] alb --> asg[Auto_Scaling_Group] asg --> azA[Instances_AZ_a] asg --> azB[Instances_AZ_","keywords":["auto","scaling","groups"]},{"url":"compute/ec2-asg-placement.html#placement","page":"EC2, ASG & placement","section":"Placement & purchase trade-offs","tool":"compute","text":"03 Placement & purchase trade-offs Cluster placement group Low latency / high PPS inside one AZ — HPC, tight chatter. Spread placement group Distinct hardware racks — reduce correlated failure (max constraints apply). Partition placement group Large distributed stores (Cassandra/Kafka-style) across ","keywords":["placement","purchase","trade","offs"]},{"url":"compute/ec2-asg-placement.html#interview","page":"EC2, ASG & placement","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"compute","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire Why Graviton on SAA scenarios? core Model answer AWS’s Arm-based instances usually deliver better price-performance for many general-purpose and compute workloads. Choose Graviton families when the OS/runtime supports Arm; fall back to x86 when binary dependencies block it. S","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"compute/elb-patterns.html","page":"Elastic Load Balancing patterns","section":"Elastic Load Balancing patterns.","tool":"compute","text":"Elastic Load Balancing patterns. Pick the balancer by protocol and feature needs: ALB for HTTP(S) L7, NLB for extreme performance / TCP-UDP / static IPs, GWLB for inline appliances. Always Multi-AZ.","keywords":["elastic","load","balancing","patterns"]},{"url":"compute/elb-patterns.html#choose","page":"Elastic Load Balancing patterns","section":"ALB vs NLB vs GWLB","tool":"compute","text":"01 ALB vs NLB vs GWLB The exam loves “which load balancer?” Map the requirement → product, then place targets in private subnets across AZs. In plain English analogy ALB is a smart receptionist who reads the URL and sends you to the right department (path/host rules). NLB is a high-speed turnstile t","keywords":["alb","vs","nlb","gwlb"]},{"url":"compute/elb-patterns.html#alb-patterns","page":"Elastic Load Balancing patterns","section":"ALB patterns that show up on SAA","tool":"compute","text":"02 ALB patterns that show up on SAA Path / host routing /api/* → API target group; app.example.com → web TG. TLS termination ACM certs on HTTPS listeners; optionally re-encrypt to targets. Sticky sessions Only when the app is not yet stateless — prefer external session store. WAF association Attach ","keywords":["alb","patterns","that","show","up","on","saa","api","app","example","com"]},{"url":"compute/elb-patterns.html#nlb-advanced","page":"Elastic Load Balancing patterns","section":"NLB & PrivateLink adjacency","tool":"compute","text":"03 NLB & PrivateLink adjacency NLB preserves source IP (with target type choices), supports TLS listeners, and fronts PrivateLink services (Endpoint Service). PrivateLink mindset: expose a service to other VPCs/accounts via interface endpoints instead of peering everything. Reach for NLB when… Need ","keywords":["nlb","privatelink","adjacency"]},{"url":"compute/elb-patterns.html#interview","page":"Elastic Load Balancing patterns","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"compute","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire When is ALB the wrong choice? core Model answer When you need static IPs , ultra-high performance L4, UDP, or PrivateLink endpoint services — choose NLB . When you need inline security appliances, choose GWLB . ALB shines at HTTP routing features. Say it out loud say it \"ALB ","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"compute/lambda-serverless.html","page":"Lambda & serverless compute","section":"Lambda & serverless compute.","tool":"compute","text":"Lambda & serverless compute. Event-driven functions that scale to zero. Know triggers, limits (15 min, concurrency, cold starts), VPC ENI implications, and when to prefer containers instead.","keywords":["lambda","serverless","compute"]},{"url":"compute/lambda-serverless.html#model","page":"Lambda & serverless compute","section":"Mental model & triggers","tool":"compute","text":"01 Mental model & triggers You ship a handler; AWS runs it on events. Pay for requests + GB-seconds . Prefer Graviton (Arm) architectures for Lambda when packages support it — better price-performance. In plain English analogy Lambda is a food truck that fires up only when an order arrives — you pay","keywords":["mental","model","triggers"]},{"url":"compute/lambda-serverless.html#limits","page":"Lambda & serverless compute","section":"Limits, cold starts & VPC","tool":"compute","text":"02 Limits, cold starts & VPC Constraint What it means Mitigation ~15 min max Long ETL doesn’t fit one invoke Step Functions, ECS/Batch, chunk work Cold start New execution environment latency Provisioned concurrency; smaller packages; snapstart (Java) Concurrency Account/function caps; noisy neighbo","keywords":["limits","cold","starts","vpc"]},{"url":"compute/lambda-serverless.html#when","page":"Lambda & serverless compute","section":"When Lambda wins vs containers","tool":"compute","text":"03 When Lambda wins vs containers Choose Lambda when… Spiky / idle-most-of-the-time workloads Glue between AWS events Short APIs and stream processors You want scale-to-zero cost Choose ECS/EKS when… Long-running or steady 24/7 services Custom runtimes / sidecars / sticky sockets Jobs > 15 minutes H","keywords":["when","lambda","wins","vs","containers"]},{"url":"compute/lambda-serverless.html#interview","page":"Lambda & serverless compute","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"compute","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire Name three hard Lambda limits for architecture. core Model answer 15-minute invocation cap, statelessness (durable state elsewhere), and cold starts / concurrency affecting latency and throughput. Those three decide when to move to containers or Step Functions. Say it out lou","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"compute/containers-ecs-eks.html","page":"Containers: ECS vs EKS","section":"Containers: ECS vs EKS.","tool":"compute","text":"Containers: ECS vs EKS. Run images from ECR on ECS (AWS-native) or EKS (managed Kubernetes). Default 2026 bias for AWS-only teams: ECS on Fargate + Graviton ; choose EKS when you need the Kubernetes ecosystem or portability.","keywords":["containers","ecs","vs","eks"]},{"url":"compute/containers-ecs-eks.html#shared","page":"Containers: ECS vs EKS","section":"Shared building blocks","tool":"compute","text":"01 Shared building blocks Both orchestrators schedule containers; neither replaces IAM. Tasks/pods get roles (task roles / IRSA / Pod Identity) — no long-lived keys in images. In plain English analogy ECS/Fargate is a fully staffed kitchen per dish — hand over the recipe, no kitchen to mop. EKS is y","keywords":["shared","building","blocks"]},{"url":"compute/containers-ecs-eks.html#ecs","page":"Containers: ECS vs EKS","section":"ECS patterns","tool":"compute","text":"02 ECS patterns task-def-sketch.json Copy { \"family\": \"web\", \"requiresCompatibilities\": [\"FARGATE\"], \"runtimePlatform\": { \"cpuArchitecture\": \"ARM64\" }, \"cpu\": \"512\", \"memory\": \"1024\", \"taskRoleArn\": \"arn:aws:iam::123:role/web-task\", \"executionRoleArn\": \"arn:aws:iam::123:role/ecs-exec\", \"containerDef","keywords":["ecs","patterns","family","web","requiresCompatibilities","FARGATE","runtimePlatform","cpuArchitecture","ARM64","cpu","512","memory","taskRoleArn","arn","aws","iam","123","role","task","executionRoleArn","exec","containerDefinitions","name","app","image","dkr","ecr","us","east","amazonaws","com","portMappings","containerPort"]},{"url":"compute/containers-ecs-eks.html#eks","page":"Containers: ECS vs EKS","section":"EKS when — and identity","tool":"compute","text":"03 EKS when — and identity Pick ECS/Fargate when… AWS-centric team, minimal K8s appetite Want least ops for long-running services Standard microservices + ALB is enough Pick EKS when… Need Helm, operators, CRDs, GitOps (Argo/Flux) Multi-cloud / on-prem portability Existing Kubernetes skills & platfo","keywords":["eks","when","and","identity"]},{"url":"compute/containers-ecs-eks.html#interview","page":"Containers: ECS vs EKS","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"compute","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire ECS vs EKS in one breath? core Model answer Both run containers from ECR. ECS is AWS-native and operationally lighter. EKS gives the Kubernetes API and ecosystem at the cost of upgrades, add-ons, and expertise. Fargate can remove node management on either. Say it out loud say","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"storage/s3-classes-and-access.html","page":"S3 storage classes & access","section":"S3 storage classes & access.","tool":"storage","text":"S3 storage classes & access. Object storage for durable blobs, data lakes, and static assets. The exam asks which class fits the access pattern — and how you lock the bucket down. As of 2026, treat Intelligent-Tiering as the default when access is unknown or changing.","keywords":["s3","storage","classes","access"]},{"url":"storage/s3-classes-and-access.html#classes","page":"S3 storage classes & access","section":"Storage classes — pick by access","tool":"storage","text":"01 Storage classes — pick by access S3 is a Regional object store (99.999999999% durability design target). You choose a storage class per object (or let lifecycle / Intelligent-Tiering move it). Cheaper classes trade retrieval cost, latency, or availability. In plain English analogy Think of a ware","keywords":["storage","classes","pick","by","access"]},{"url":"storage/s3-classes-and-access.html#lifecycle","page":"S3 storage classes & access","section":"Lifecycle, versioning & replication","tool":"storage","text":"02 Lifecycle, versioning & replication Lifecycle rules Transition classes or expire objects/versions after N days. Pair with Intelligent-Tiering or explicit IA/Glacier transitions. Versioning Keeps prior object versions; protects against overwrite/delete. Enables MFA Delete and Object Lock patterns.","keywords":["lifecycle","versioning","replication"]},{"url":"storage/s3-classes-and-access.html#access","page":"S3 storage classes & access","section":"Access control & private paths","tool":"storage","text":"03 Access control & private paths Block Public Access on (account + bucket). Prefer bucket policies + IAM roles over ACLs. Public websites go through CloudFront (OAC), not an open bucket. Anti-pattern Public bucket for “simple static site” Long-lived access keys in the app ACLs as the primary contro","keywords":["access","control","private","paths"]},{"url":"storage/s3-classes-and-access.html#interview","page":"S3 storage classes & access","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"storage","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire When do you choose Intelligent-Tiering vs Standard vs Glacier? core Model answer Standard for hot, predictable frequent access. Intelligent-Tiering when access is unknown or changes over time — it auto-moves between tiers without retrieval fees for those moves (you pay a smal","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"storage/ebs-efs-fsx.html","page":"EBS, EFS & FSx","section":"EBS, EFS & FSx.","tool":"storage","text":"EBS, EFS & FSx. Three ways to attach durable bytes to compute: a disk for one instance (EBS), a shared NFS (EFS), or a managed specialty filesystem (FSx). The exam is almost always “which shape, and why?”","keywords":["ebs","efs","fsx"]},{"url":"storage/ebs-efs-fsx.html#compare","page":"EBS, EFS & FSx","section":"Pick the storage shape","tool":"storage","text":"01 Pick the storage shape S3 = objects over HTTPS. EBS = block volume for (usually) one EC2. EFS = elastic NFS many clients mount. FSx = managed Windows / Lustre / NetApp / OpenZFS when you need those protocols or performance profiles. In plain English analogy EBS is the hard drive bolted into one l","keywords":["pick","the","storage","shape"]},{"url":"storage/ebs-efs-fsx.html#ebs","page":"EBS, EFS & FSx","section":"EBS deep cut","tool":"storage","text":"02 EBS deep cut gp3 General-purpose SSD default — baseline IOPS/throughput tunable independently of size. io2 / io2 Block Express Highest IOPS & durability; Multi-Attach for clustered apps that support shared block. Snapshots Incremental, stored in S3 behind the scenes; copy across Regions for DR. E","keywords":["ebs","deep","cut"]},{"url":"storage/ebs-efs-fsx.html#efs-fsx","page":"EBS, EFS & FSx","section":"EFS & FSx","tool":"storage","text":"03 EFS & FSx EFS Regional, Multi-AZ by default Elastic / provisioned throughput modes Lifecycle → Infrequent Access / Archive Mount targets per AZ; NFS locks FSx family Windows File Server — SMB, AD Lustre — HPC, often linked to S3 NetApp ONTAP — multiprotocol, snapshots OpenZFS — NFS with ZFS featu","keywords":["efs","fsx"]},{"url":"storage/ebs-efs-fsx.html#interview","page":"EBS, EFS & FSx","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"storage","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire S3 vs EBS vs EFS — one sentence each? warm-up Model answer S3 is object storage over the API. EBS is a block disk for one EC2 in one AZ. EFS is a shared NFS many instances mount across AZs. Say it out loud say it \"S3 objects, EBS one VM's disk, EFS shared drive across many VM","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"storage/aws-backup.html","page":"AWS Backup & protection","section":"AWS Backup & protection.","tool":"storage","text":"AWS Backup & protection. Central policies for backups across EBS, EFS, RDS, DynamoDB, FSx, and more — vaults, retention, cross-account/Region copies, and immutability with Vault Lock. On the exam, “centralized backup plan” usually means AWS Backup.","keywords":["aws","backup","protection"]},{"url":"storage/aws-backup.html#model","page":"AWS Backup & protection","section":"Mental model","tool":"storage","text":"01 Mental model A backup plan schedules what to protect; a vault holds recovery points; tags / resource assignments select targets. AWS Backup orchestrates native snapshots/exports so you don't glue cron + scripts per service. In plain English analogy Think of a corporate records room. The plan is t","keywords":["mental","model"]},{"url":"storage/aws-backup.html#features","page":"AWS Backup & protection","section":"Exam-critical features","tool":"storage","text":"02 Exam-critical features Lifecycle in the plan Transition cold storage / expire recovery points to match RPO and cost. Cross-Region / cross-account Copy rules for DR and blast-radius isolation (org backup account pattern). Vault Lock WORM retention on the vault — compliance scenarios. PITR Point-in","keywords":["exam","critical","features"]},{"url":"storage/aws-backup.html#design","page":"AWS Backup & protection","section":"Design checklist","tool":"storage","text":"03 Design checklist Map RPO/RTO → schedule frequency and restore testing cadence. Tag production resources; assign plans by tag — don't list ARNs forever. Send copies to a dedicated backup account with tighter IAM. Enable Vault Lock only after a cooling-off / compliance review — it's hard to undo. P","keywords":["design","checklist"]},{"url":"storage/aws-backup.html#interview","page":"AWS Backup & protection","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"storage","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What problem does AWS Backup solve? warm-up Model answer It centralizes backup plans, vaults, and restores across many AWS resource types so you apply one policy (tags, schedules, retention, copies) instead of per-service snapshot scripts. Say it out loud say it \"One backup p","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"databases/rds-aurora-ha.html","page":"RDS & Aurora high availability","section":"RDS & Aurora high availability.","tool":"databases","text":"RDS & Aurora high availability. Managed relational engines with Multi-AZ failover, read replicas, and Aurora's shared storage. As of 2026, for spiky or unpredictable relational load, prefer Aurora Serverless v2 (ACUs) over older Serverless v1 thinking.","keywords":["rds","aurora","high","availability"]},{"url":"databases/rds-aurora-ha.html#rds-vs-aurora","page":"RDS & Aurora high availability","section":"RDS vs Aurora","tool":"databases","text":"01 RDS vs Aurora RDS runs engines (Postgres, MySQL, SQL Server, …) on managed instances with automated backups. Aurora is AWS's cloud-native MySQL/Postgres-compatible engine with storage that auto-grows and replicates across AZs. In plain English analogy RDS Multi-AZ is a primary cashier with a trai","keywords":["rds","vs","aurora"]},{"url":"databases/rds-aurora-ha.html#ha","page":"RDS & Aurora high availability","section":"HA patterns","tool":"databases","text":"02 HA patterns flowchart TD app[AppTier] --> writer[WriterEndpoint] writer --> primary[PrimaryInstance] primary -->|syncMultiAZ| standby[StandbyAZ] primary -->|async| replica[ReadReplica] app --> reader[ReaderEndpoint] reader --> replica Multi-AZ Synchronous standby in another AZ. Failover on host/A","keywords":["ha","patterns"]},{"url":"databases/rds-aurora-ha.html#serverless-v2","page":"RDS & Aurora high availability","section":"Aurora Serverless v2 (2026 default)","tool":"databases","text":"03 Aurora Serverless v2 (2026 default) Serverless v2 scales Aurora Capacity Units (ACUs) up and down in fine increments, can mix with provisioned instances in the same cluster, and fits variable / unpredictable relational workloads without the coarse pauses of older Serverless v1. Set min/max ACUs t","keywords":["aurora","serverless","v2","default"]},{"url":"databases/rds-aurora-ha.html#interview","page":"RDS & Aurora high availability","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"databases","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire RDS Multi-AZ vs read replica? core Model answer Multi-AZ is a sync standby for automatic failover — HA, not read traffic. Read replicas are async copies that serve reads and can be promoted; use them for scale-out and cross-Region DR. Say it out loud say it \"Multi-AZ keeps me","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"databases/dynamodb-access-patterns.html","page":"DynamoDB access patterns","section":"DynamoDB access patterns.","tool":"databases","text":"DynamoDB access patterns. Key-value / document store that scales with the right partition key design. As of 2026, prefer on-demand capacity when traffic is unpredictable; use provisioned + auto scaling when steady and cost-sensitive.","keywords":["dynamodb","access","patterns"]},{"url":"databases/dynamodb-access-patterns.html#keys","page":"DynamoDB access patterns","section":"Keys & access patterns first","tool":"databases","text":"01 Keys & access patterns first You design tables around how you query , not around normalized ERDs. Every item needs a partition key (and optional sort key ). Hot partitions and table-scan habits fail both the exam and production. In plain English analogy DynamoDB is a giant set of labeled filing c","keywords":["keys","access","patterns","first"]},{"url":"databases/dynamodb-access-patterns.html#capacity","page":"DynamoDB access patterns","section":"On-demand vs provisioned (2026)","tool":"databases","text":"02 On-demand vs provisioned (2026) On-demand Default for spiky / unknown Pay per request No capacity planning Great for new apps & events Provisioned Steady, predictable RCU/WCU Auto Scaling for gentle drift Often cheaper at high constant load Reserved capacity options Common trap gotcha Strongly co","keywords":["on","demand","vs","provisioned"]},{"url":"databases/dynamodb-access-patterns.html#features","page":"DynamoDB access patterns","section":"Adjacent features","tool":"databases","text":"03 Adjacent features DAX Microsecond cache for eventually consistent reads — when ElastiCache-style caching is in-process for Dynamo. Streams Change data capture → Lambda, analytics, fan-out. TTL Expire items automatically (sessions, temp data). Global Tables Multi-Region active-active with conflict","keywords":["adjacent","features"]},{"url":"databases/dynamodb-access-patterns.html#interview","page":"DynamoDB access patterns","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"databases","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire How do you design a DynamoDB table? core Model answer List access patterns first. Choose a partition key that spreads load and a sort key for range queries. Add GSIs only for additional patterns. Prefer Query/GetItem over Scan. Pick on-demand if traffic is unknown; provisione","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"databases/elasticache.html","page":"ElastiCache","section":"ElastiCache.","tool":"databases","text":"ElastiCache. Managed in-memory cache (Redis / Valkey-compatible and Memcached) to cut database load and latency. The exam asks engine choice, HA, and when cache belongs in front of RDS/DynamoDB vs when the DB feature (DAX) is enough.","keywords":["elasticache"]},{"url":"databases/elasticache.html#when","page":"ElastiCache","section":"When caching wins","tool":"databases","text":"01 When caching wins Put ElastiCache in front of a relational or document store for hot keys , session stores, leaderboards, and read-heavy pages. Invalidate or TTL carefully — stale cache is a silent bug. In plain English analogy Your database is the library stacks. ElastiCache is the cart of books","keywords":["when","caching","wins"]},{"url":"databases/elasticache.html#engines","page":"ElastiCache","section":"Redis vs Memcached","tool":"databases","text":"02 Redis vs Memcached Redis / Valkey Rich data structures, persistence options Replication + Multi-AZ failover Cluster mode for scale-out Pub/Sub, Lua, sorted sets Memcached Simple key/value Multithreaded, easy horizontal scale No fancy structures / persistence story When you only need a dumb cache ","keywords":["redis","vs","memcached"]},{"url":"databases/elasticache.html#patterns","page":"ElastiCache","section":"Patterns & HA","tool":"databases","text":"03 Patterns & HA Cache-aside App reads cache → miss → DB → populate cache. Most common exam pattern. Session store Sticky-user sessions in Redis so ASG instances stay stateless. Multi-AZ Redis Primary + replica with automatic failover for HA. vs DAX DAX is DynamoDB-specific microsecond cache; Elasti","keywords":["patterns","ha"]},{"url":"databases/elasticache.html#interview","page":"ElastiCache","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"databases","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire Redis vs Memcached on ElastiCache? warm-up Model answer Redis for rich structures, replication/failover, and advanced features. Memcached for simple, multithreaded key/value caching when you don't need those features. Say it out loud say it \"Redis for features and HA; Memcach","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"integration/sqs-sns-eventbridge.html","page":"SQS, SNS & EventBridge","section":"SQS, SNS & EventBridge.","tool":"integration","text":"SQS, SNS & EventBridge. Decouple producers and consumers. Queues buffer work, topics fan out notifications, and the event bus routes events by rule. As of 2026, treat EventBridge as the default for application and SaaS events (not “raw CloudWatch Events” as the mental model).","keywords":["sqs","sns","eventbridge"]},{"url":"integration/sqs-sns-eventbridge.html#choose","page":"SQS, SNS & EventBridge","section":"Which primitive?","tool":"integration","text":"01 Which primitive? SQS = pull queue (buffer, retry, DLQ). SNS = push fan-out to many subscribers. EventBridge = content-based routing across buses, SaaS partners, and schedules. In plain English analogy SQS is a ticket spike at a deli — workers pull the next order when free. SNS is the PA system an","keywords":["which","primitive"]},{"url":"integration/sqs-sns-eventbridge.html#sqs-sns","page":"SQS, SNS & EventBridge","section":"SQS & SNS details","tool":"integration","text":"02 SQS & SNS details Standard vs FIFO SQS Standard = best-effort ordering, nearly unlimited throughput. FIFO = exactly-once processing within a group + ordered — use when the stem demands order. Visibility timeout Hide in-flight messages while processing; set ≥ worst-case work time. DLQ After maxRec","keywords":["sqs","sns","details"]},{"url":"integration/sqs-sns-eventbridge.html#eventbridge","page":"SQS, SNS & EventBridge","section":"EventBridge as the 2026 default","tool":"integration","text":"03 EventBridge as the 2026 default Prefer EventBridge for application events, AWS service events, partner SaaS events, and Scheduler one-offs/crons. Rules filter on content; targets include Lambda, SQS, SNS, Step Functions, API destinations. Default bus for AWS events; custom buses for your domain e","keywords":["eventbridge","as","the","default"]},{"url":"integration/sqs-sns-eventbridge.html#interview","page":"SQS, SNS & EventBridge","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"integration","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire SQS vs SNS vs EventBridge? core Model answer SQS buffers work for competing consumers with retries/DLQ. SNS push-fan-outs one message to many subscribers. EventBridge routes events with rules — the 2026 default for app/SaaS/AWS events and schedules. Combine them: EventBridge/","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"integration/api-gateway-step-functions.html","page":"API Gateway & Step Functions","section":"API Gateway & Step Functions.","tool":"integration","text":"API Gateway & Step Functions. Front doors for HTTP/WebSocket APIs, and durable workflows that orchestrate Lambda, services, and human approvals. The exam separates request/response APIs from long-running orchestration .","keywords":["api","gateway","step","functions"]},{"url":"integration/api-gateway-step-functions.html#api-gw","page":"API Gateway & Step Functions","section":"API Gateway flavors","tool":"integration","text":"01 API Gateway flavors HTTP APIs = cheaper/faster for most Lambda/JWT proxies. REST APIs = full feature set (API keys, WAF-heavy enterprise patterns, usage plans). WebSocket APIs = bidirectional chat/push. In plain English analogy API Gateway is the hotel front desk — auth, rate limits, and routing ","keywords":["api","gateway","flavors"]},{"url":"integration/api-gateway-step-functions.html#step-functions","page":"API Gateway & Step Functions","section":"Step Functions","tool":"integration","text":"02 Step Functions flowchart TD start[Start] --> task1[TaskLambda] task1 --> choice{Success} choice -->|yes| task2[TaskService] choice -->|no| retry[RetryThenCatch] retry --> dlq[AlertOrSQS] task2 --> wait[WaitOrCallback] wait --> endNode[End] Standard workflows Long-running (up to 1 year), exactly-o","keywords":["step","functions"]},{"url":"integration/api-gateway-step-functions.html#together","page":"API Gateway & Step Functions","section":"Combining the two","tool":"integration","text":"03 Combining the two API Gateway → Lambda for simple request/response. API Gateway → Step Functions (sync/async start) for multi-step workflows. EventBridge rule → Step Functions for event-driven orchestration. Express + EventBridge for high-volume pipelines; Standard for durable sagas. Loan applica","keywords":["combining","the","two"]},{"url":"integration/api-gateway-step-functions.html#interview","page":"API Gateway & Step Functions","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"integration","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire HTTP API vs REST API on API Gateway? warm-up Model answer HTTP APIs are cheaper and lower latency for common Lambda/JWT proxy cases. REST APIs unlock richer features (usage plans, API keys, more middleware-style options). Pick HTTP unless you need REST-only features. Say it o","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"resilience/multi-az-multi-region.html","page":"Multi-AZ vs multi-Region","section":"Survive a zone. Survive a region.","tool":"resilience","text":"Survive a zone. Survive a region. Multi-AZ is the default HA pattern inside one Region. Multi-Region is for disasters that take the whole Region down — rarer, slower to build, and much more expensive. The exam wants you to pick the right blast radius.","keywords":["survive","zone","region"]},{"url":"resilience/multi-az-multi-region.html#az-vs-region","page":"Multi-AZ vs multi-Region","section":"AZ vs Region — blast radius","tool":"resilience","text":"01 AZ vs Region — blast radius An Availability Zone is one or more data centers with independent power and networking. A Region is a geographic cluster of AZs. Losing an AZ ≠ losing a Region. In plain English analogy Think of a city with three power substations (AZs) and a whole metro area (Region).","keywords":["az","vs","region","blast","radius"]},{"url":"resilience/multi-az-multi-region.html#patterns","page":"Multi-AZ vs multi-Region","section":"Service patterns that get Multi-AZ \"for free\"","tool":"resilience","text":"02 Service patterns that get Multi-AZ \"for free\" Spread compute and data across AZs; let AWS manage failover where it can. ALB + ASG / ECS Targets in ≥2 AZs; health checks drain bad nodes. Subnets must span AZs. RDS Multi-AZ Synchronous standby in another AZ; automatic failover (~1–2 min). Not a rea","keywords":["service","patterns","that","get","multi","az","for","free"]},{"url":"resilience/multi-az-multi-region.html#when-region","page":"Multi-AZ vs multi-Region","section":"When the exam wants multi-Region","tool":"resilience","text":"03 When the exam wants multi-Region Choose multi-Region when the stem says Region failure , compliance data residency in two places , or global users with low latency — not for routine AZ HA. S3 Cross-Region Replication + versioning for object DR. Aurora Global Database / DynamoDB Global Tables for ","keywords":["when","the","exam","wants","multi","region"]},{"url":"resilience/multi-az-multi-region.html#interview","page":"Multi-AZ vs multi-Region","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"resilience","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Multi-AZ vs multi-Region — one sentence each. warm-up Model answer Multi-AZ keeps the app running when one data center / AZ fails inside a Region. Multi-Region keeps (or restores) the app when an entire Region is unavailable — more replication, DNS, and cost. Say it out loud say i","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"resilience/dr-rpo-rto.html","page":"DR strategies, RPO & RTO","section":"DR is a budget, not a checkbox.","tool":"resilience","text":"DR is a budget, not a checkbox. RPO is how much data you can lose. RTO is how long you can be down. Every DR strategy on SAA-C03 is really \"how warm is the standby?\" — and warmer always costs more.","keywords":["dr","is","budget","not","checkbox"]},{"url":"resilience/dr-rpo-rto.html#objectives","page":"DR strategies, RPO & RTO","section":"RPO vs RTO","tool":"resilience","text":"01 RPO vs RTO Business sets the targets; architecture spends money to meet them. Lower numbers → continuous replication, warm/hot standbys, multi-Region. In plain English analogy RPO is how far back your undo button goes (last backup vs live mirror). RTO is how long until the store reopens after a f","keywords":["rpo","vs","rto"]},{"url":"resilience/dr-rpo-rto.html#strategies","page":"DR strategies, RPO & RTO","section":"The four DR strategies","tool":"resilience","text":"02 The four DR strategies From cheapest/slowest to most expensive/fastest. Match the stem's RPO/RTO language to a tier. flowchart LR backup[Backup_and_Restore] --> pilot[Pilot_Light] pilot --> warm[Warm_Standby] warm --> multi[Multi_Site_Active] Strategy What you run Typical RTO/RPO feel Backup & re","keywords":["the","four","dr","strategies"]},{"url":"resilience/dr-rpo-rto.html#aws-building","page":"DR strategies, RPO & RTO","section":"AWS building blocks","tool":"resilience","text":"03 AWS building blocks AWS Backup Central policies across EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS, etc. Still restore-test. RDS / Aurora Automated backups + PITR (RPO); Multi-AZ (RTO in-Region); cross-Region replica / Aurora Global (Region DR). S3 Versioning + CRR; Object Lock for immutability when ransomware is in","keywords":["aws","building","blocks"]},{"url":"resilience/dr-rpo-rto.html#interview","page":"DR strategies, RPO & RTO","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"resilience","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Define RPO and RTO in one breath. warm-up Model answer RPO = maximum acceptable data loss (time). RTO = maximum acceptable downtime. Architecture and spend follow those two numbers. Say it out loud say it \"RPO is data; RTO is clocks. Tighter either way costs more.\" Pilot light vs ","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"resilience/decoupling-and-scaling.html","page":"Decoupling & scaling patterns","section":"Queues absorb shock. Autoscaling absorbs load.","tool":"resilience","text":"Queues absorb shock. Autoscaling absorbs load. Tight coupling turns one slow dependency into a site-wide outage. SQS / SNS / EventBridge and ASG / Lambda concurrency let producers and consumers fail and scale independently — a constant SAA theme.","keywords":["queues","absorb","shock","autoscaling","absorbs","load"]},{"url":"resilience/decoupling-and-scaling.html#why-decouple","page":"Decoupling & scaling patterns","section":"Why decoupling wins","tool":"resilience","text":"01 Why decoupling wins If service A calls B synchronously and B is down, A is down. A queue or topic makes the failure temporary backlog instead of user-facing errors. In plain English analogy A restaurant ticket rail is a queue: the kitchen can spike without the host crashing. A megaphone to all co","keywords":["why","decoupling","wins"]},{"url":"resilience/decoupling-and-scaling.html#messaging","page":"Decoupling & scaling patterns","section":"Pick the right messaging tool","tool":"resilience","text":"02 Pick the right messaging tool SQS Pull queue between producers and workers. Standard = at-least-once + best-effort order. FIFO = exactly-once processing + order per message group. SNS Push pub/sub fan-out to many subscribers (Lambda, SQS, HTTP, email). Use SNS→SQS for durable fan-out. EventBridge","keywords":["pick","the","right","messaging","tool"]},{"url":"resilience/decoupling-and-scaling.html#scaling","page":"Decoupling & scaling patterns","section":"Scaling patterns","tool":"resilience","text":"03 Scaling patterns ASG on CPU, ALB request count, or SQS ApproximateNumberOfMessages — classic worker farm. Target tracking policies over manual step thresholds when the stem wants \"automatically maintain.\" Lambda scales with concurrency; use reserved concurrency to protect downstream. DynamoDB on-","keywords":["scaling","patterns"]},{"url":"resilience/decoupling-and-scaling.html#interview","page":"Decoupling & scaling patterns","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"resilience","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire SQS vs SNS vs EventBridge — when? core Model answer SQS = buffer work for consumers. SNS = fan-out the same message to many endpoints. EventBridge = route events with rules/filters across apps and AWS services. Combine SNS→SQS for durable multi-consumer. Say it out loud say it \"Qu","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"cost-ops/pricing-models.html","page":"Pricing models","section":"Pay for what you use — then commit where it sticks.","tool":"cost-ops","text":"Pay for what you use — then commit where it sticks. On-Demand is flexible. Savings Plans / Reserved Instances discount steady load. Spot discounts interruptible work. Graviton and right-sizing beat buying a bigger box. The exam rewards matching commitment to workload shape.","keywords":["pay","for","what","you","use","then","commit","where","it","sticks"]},{"url":"cost-ops/pricing-models.html#models","page":"Pricing models","section":"The pricing menu","tool":"cost-ops","text":"01 The pricing menu Pick the model from the stem's keywords: steady → commit; spiky/unknown → On-Demand or serverless; batch/fault-tolerant → Spot. In plain English analogy On-Demand is a walk-up hotel rate. Savings Plans / RIs are a corporate rate for nights you know you'll stay. Spot is a standby ","keywords":["the","pricing","menu"]},{"url":"cost-ops/pricing-models.html#tactics","page":"Pricing models","section":"Cost levers beyond the price tag","tool":"cost-ops","text":"02 Cost levers beyond the price tag Right-size CloudWatch + Compute Optimizer → smaller / fewer instances before you buy a Savings Plan. Graviton ARM instances often better price/performance — prefer when compatible. S3 classes Intelligent-Tiering / lifecycle to IA / Glacier for cold data. Data tran","keywords":["cost","levers","beyond","the","price","tag"]},{"url":"cost-ops/pricing-models.html#exam-signals","page":"Pricing models","section":"Stem signals → answer","tool":"cost-ops","text":"03 Stem signals → answer \"Minimize cost\" + fault-tolerant batch → Spot (+ checkpoint / ASG mixed instances). \"Predictable\" / \"steady state\" → Savings Plans or RI . \"Unpredictable traffic\" / \"rarely used\" → On-Demand or serverless . \"License tied to cores / sockets\" → Dedicated Host . \"Infrequently a","keywords":["stem","signals","answer"]},{"url":"cost-ops/pricing-models.html#interview","page":"Pricing models","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"cost-ops","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Savings Plans vs Reserved Instances? core Model answer Both discount committed usage. Compute Savings Plans are the flexible modern default ($/hr across instance family, size, Region options, and Fargate/Lambda). RIs lock more narrowly to instance attributes — still valid, less fl","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cost-explorer-budgets.html","page":"Cost Explorer, Budgets & allocation","section":"See the bill. Cap the bill. Own the bill.","tool":"cost-ops","text":"See the bill. Cap the bill. Own the bill. Cost Explorer explains spend. Budgets alerts (and can act) when you cross a line. Allocation tags + CUR assign dollars to teams. Without visibility, every other cost optimization is guessing.","keywords":["see","the","bill","cap","own"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cost-explorer-budgets.html#explorer","page":"Cost Explorer, Budgets & allocation","section":"Cost Explorer","tool":"cost-ops","text":"01 Cost Explorer Interactive charts and filters: by service, linked account, tag, Region, usage type. Use it to find the spike before you redesign the architecture. In plain English analogy Cost Explorer is your bank app's spending categories. Budgets are the text that says \"you've hit 80% of grocer","keywords":["cost","explorer"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cost-explorer-budgets.html#budgets","page":"Cost Explorer, Budgets & allocation","section":"AWS Budgets","tool":"cost-ops","text":"02 AWS Budgets Set cost, usage, or Savings Plans / RI coverage budgets. Notify via SNS/email; optionally trigger actions (e.g. apply an IAM policy to deny expensive launches — used carefully). Good Budgets use Alert at 50% / 80% / 100% forecasted Per account or tagged project Usage budgets for data ","keywords":["aws","budgets"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cost-explorer-budgets.html#allocation","page":"Cost Explorer, Budgets & allocation","section":"Allocation & multi-account","tool":"cost-ops","text":"03 Allocation & multi-account User-defined cost allocation tags — e.g. Project , Environment , Team . AWS Organizations + linked accounts → Cost Explorer by account; SCPs limit blast radius. CUR (Cost and Usage Report) to S3 for Athena/QuickSight when Explorer isn't enough. Cost Anomaly Detection fo","keywords":["allocation","multi","account","Project","Environment","Team"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cost-explorer-budgets.html#interview","page":"Cost Explorer, Budgets & allocation","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"cost-ops","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Cost Explorer vs Budgets? warm-up Model answer Explorer analyzes historical and forecasted spend. Budgets watches thresholds and notifies (or acts). You usually need both: explore to understand, budget to stay honest. Say it out loud say it \"Explorer is the microscope; Budgets is ","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cloudwatch-config-cloudtrail.html","page":"CloudWatch, Config & CloudTrail","section":"Metrics, config truth, and an audit trail.","tool":"cost-ops","text":"Metrics, config truth, and an audit trail. CloudWatch watches health and performance. Config records what resources look like and whether they comply. CloudTrail records who did what via the API. Different questions — don't swap them on the exam.","keywords":["metrics","config","truth","and","an","audit","trail"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cloudwatch-config-cloudtrail.html#triangle","page":"CloudWatch, Config & CloudTrail","section":"Three tools, three questions","tool":"cost-ops","text":"01 Three tools, three questions \"Is it healthy?\" → CloudWatch. \"Is it configured correctly / what changed?\" → Config. \"Who called the API?\" → CloudTrail. In plain English analogy CloudWatch is the car's dashboard (speed, temp). Config is the inspection checklist (did someone remove the seatbelts?). ","keywords":["three","tools","questions"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cloudwatch-config-cloudtrail.html#cloudwatch","page":"CloudWatch, Config & CloudTrail","section":"CloudWatch essentials","tool":"cost-ops","text":"02 CloudWatch essentials Metrics & alarms CPU, ALB 5xx, custom metrics → alarm → SNS / Auto Scaling / incident chat. Logs Log groups; metric filters turn log patterns into alarms. Dashboards Ops visibility; not a substitute for alarms. Events → EventBridge Schedule and react to AWS events (modern ho","keywords":["cloudwatch","essentials"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cloudwatch-config-cloudtrail.html#config-trail","page":"CloudWatch, Config & CloudTrail","section":"Config & CloudTrail","tool":"cost-ops","text":"03 Config & CloudTrail Config — continuous inventory + history; Config Rules (managed or custom) for \"S3 must be encrypted,\" \"SG must not be 0.0.0.0/0,\" etc. Conformance packs for packs of rules. CloudTrail — management (and optional data) events; multi-Region trail to S3; org trail from the managem","keywords":["config","cloudtrail"]},{"url":"cost-ops/cloudwatch-config-cloudtrail.html#interview","page":"CloudWatch, Config & CloudTrail","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"cost-ops","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire CloudWatch vs CloudTrail vs Config — one line each. warm-up Model answer CloudWatch = metrics/logs/alarms (health). CloudTrail = API activity audit. Config = resource configuration history + compliance rules. Say it out loud say it \"Dashboard, camera, and inspection checklist — do","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html","page":"SAA-C03 domain map","section":"Four domains. One architecture brain.","tool":"exam","text":"Four domains. One architecture brain. SAA-C03 scores you on Secure · Resilient · High-performing · Cost-optimized . This map shows which guide modules feed each domain — study by domain when a mock exposes a weak area.","keywords":["four","domains","one","architecture","brain"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#domains","page":"SAA-C03 domain map","section":"Official domains at a glance","tool":"exam","text":"01 Official domains at a glance Domain Weight (approx.) What \"good\" looks like 1 · Design Secure Architectures ~30% IAM least privilege, encryption, network isolation, edge protection, detective controls 2 · Design Resilient Architectures ~26% Multi-AZ, decoupling, DR/RPO/RTO, backups, failover 3 · ","keywords":["official","domains","at","glance"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#map","page":"SAA-C03 domain map","section":"Domains → modules","tool":"exam","text":"02 Domains → modules flowchart TB subgraph d1[Domain1_Secure] iam[identity-security] netsec[networking_SG_endpoints] enc[KMS_secrets] end subgraph d2[Domain2_Resilient] res[resilience] dbha[databases_HA] integ[integration_queues] end subgraph d3[Domain3_HighPerf] cmp[compute] sto[storage] edge[Cloud","keywords":["domains","modules"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#method","page":"SAA-C03 domain map","section":"How to use this map","tool":"exam","text":"03 How to use this map Finish Exam method so you know how stems hide the domain. Run the timed mock — note weak domain bars. Drill that domain's modules, then the scenario bank filtered mentally by chip. Night-before: cheat-sheet + flashcards .","keywords":["how","to","use","this","map"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#check","page":"SAA-C03 domain map","section":"Quick self-check","tool":"exam","text":"04 Quick self-check Stem: \"encrypt at rest and in transit, least privilege.\" Domain? warm-up Answer Secure (Domain 1) — KMS, TLS, IAM roles. Cost/perf may appear as distractors. Stem: \"RTO 15 min, survive Region failure.\" Domain? core Answer Resilient (Domain 2) — multi-Region DR warmth. Secure encr","keywords":["quick","self","check"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html","page":"Architecture scenario bank","section":"Architecture scenarios, SAA style.","tool":"exam","text":"Architecture scenarios, SAA style. Twenty design stems across Secure · Resilient · High-performing · Cost . Expand each card: best answer, why, and the domain chip. Reason out loud before you open.","keywords":["architecture","scenarios","saa","style"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#secure","page":"Architecture scenario bank","section":"Secure architectures","tool":"exam","text":"01 Secure architectures App on EC2 must call S3 without long-lived keys on the instance. Secure Best answer Attach an IAM role to the instance (instance profile) with least-privilege S3 permissions; use the AWS SDK default credential chain. Why Roles issue temporary credentials via IMDS. Access keys","keywords":["secure","architectures","s3","CreateBucket"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#resilient","page":"Architecture scenario bank","section":"Resilient architectures","tool":"exam","text":"02 Resilient architectures Survive AZ failure for a three-tier web app with RDS. Resilient Best answer ALB + ASG across ≥2 AZs; RDS Multi-AZ ; subnets in multiple AZs. Why Multi-AZ is the default HA pattern. Multi-Region is overkill if only AZ failure is in scope. RTO < 1 hour, RPO near-zero, tolera","keywords":["resilient","architectures"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#perf","page":"Architecture scenario bank","section":"High-performing architectures","tool":"exam","text":"03 High-performing architectures Global users, mostly static assets, lowest latency. High-performing Best answer S3 origin + CloudFront (optional OAC); cache at edge. Why Edge caches cut origin load and RTT. Multi-Region active app stacks are overkill for static. Read-heavy relational DB; writes are","keywords":["high","performing","architectures"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#cost","page":"Architecture scenario bank","section":"Cost-optimized architectures","tool":"exam","text":"04 Cost-optimized architectures Steady 24×7 EC2/Fargate fleet; maximize savings with flexibility. Cost Best answer Compute Savings Plans (after right-sizing); prefer Graviton where possible. Why Steady → commit. Compute SP beats rigid RI when families may change. Fault-tolerant nightly analytics job","keywords":["cost","optimized","architectures"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#mixed","page":"Architecture scenario bank","section":"Mixed / trick stems","tool":"exam","text":"05 Mixed / trick stems Secure + resilient: public web, private app/DB, Multi-AZ. Secure Best answer Public subnets: ALB only. Private: app ASG. DB: private Multi-AZ RDS; SG least privilege; no bastion keys if SSM Session Manager fits. Why Classic three-tier VPC — security groups as primary firewall,","keywords":["mixed","trick","stems"]},{"url":"exam/mock-exam.html","page":"Timed mock exam","section":"30-minute SAA-style mock.","tool":"exam","text":"30-minute SAA-style mock. Twenty domain-weighted questions (~5 Secure, ~5 Resilient, ~5 High-performing, ~5 Cost). Start the timer, pick one answer each, then score by domain and revisit weak lessons.","keywords":["minute","saa","style","mock"]},{"url":"exam/mock-exam.html#take","page":"Timed mock exam","section":"Take the mock","tool":"exam","text":"01 Take the mock Treat it like the real exam: no peeking at notes. After scoring, use the domain bars to choose the next module from the domain map .","keywords":["take","the","mock"]},{"url":"exam/mock-exam.html#after","page":"Timed mock exam","section":"After you score","tool":"exam","text":"02 After you score Secure weak → IAM , KMS , endpoints . Resilient weak → Multi-AZ , DR , decoupling . Perf weak → ELB , S3 , ElastiCache . Cost weak → pricing , Budgets . Then drill the scenario bank and flashcards .","keywords":["after","you","score"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html","page":"SAA cheat-sheet","section":"Night-before SAA versus tables.","tool":"exam","text":"Night-before SAA versus tables. Dense rapid-recall: load balancers, storage classes, HA scopes, messaging, identity, and pricing. Skim once; quiz yourself from the left column.","keywords":["night","before","saa","versus","tables"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#lb-compute","page":"SAA cheat-sheet","section":"Load balancing & compute","tool":"exam","text":"01 Load balancing & compute ALB vs NLB vs GWLB ALB (L7) HTTP/HTTPS, path/host rules WAF integration Microservices / containers NLB (L4) TCP/UDP/TLS Ultra-low latency Static IPs / PrivateLink GWLB = third-party appliances inline. Classic LB = legacy distractor. Compute pick EC2 + ASG Full control, st","keywords":["load","balancing","compute"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#storage","page":"SAA cheat-sheet","section":"Storage classes & block/file","tool":"exam","text":"02 Storage classes & block/file S3 storage classes Class Use Standard Hot, frequent access Intelligent-Tiering Unknown/changing access Standard-IA / One Zone-IA Infrequent; One Zone = cheaper, not Multi-AZ Glacier Instant Archive, ms retrieve Glacier Flexible / Deep Archive Hours retrieve; cheapest ","keywords":["storage","classes","block","file"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#ha-dr","page":"SAA cheat-sheet","section":"Multi-AZ vs multi-Region & DR","tool":"exam","text":"03 Multi-AZ vs multi-Region & DR Multi-AZ Survive one AZ RDS Multi-AZ, ALB/ASG spread Default HA answer Multi-Region Survive Region event CRR, Global Tables, Aurora Global Route 53 failover DR tier Warmth Cost Backup & restore Cold $ Pilot light Data hot, compute cold $$ Warm standby Scaled-down ful","keywords":["multi","az","vs","region","dr"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#messaging-identity","page":"SAA cheat-sheet","section":"Messaging & identity","tool":"exam","text":"04 Messaging & identity SQS vs SNS vs EventBridge SQS Queue / buffer Pull consumers Standard vs FIFO SNS Pub/sub fan-out Push to many SNS→SQS durable EventBridge = bus + rules + SaaS partners + schedules. IAM quick hits User vs role Prefer roles + federation; avoid long-lived keys. Policy types Iden","keywords":["messaging","identity"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#cost-db","page":"SAA cheat-sheet","section":"Cost, DB & ops","tool":"exam","text":"05 Cost, DB & ops Pricing cues Steady Savings Plans / RI + Graviton Batch / FT Spot Spiky idle Lambda / serverless License cores Dedicated Host Databases RDS Multi-AZ HA standby ≠ read scale Read replica Read scale / cross-Region DR seed Aurora Storage Multi-AZ; fast failover DynamoDB Key-value; on-","keywords":["cost","db","ops"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#check","page":"SAA cheat-sheet","section":"Last-minute traps","tool":"exam","text":"06 Last-minute traps One Zone-IA is not Multi-AZ durable. NAT for private→internet; VPC endpoint when \"no internet / cheaper to S3/Dynamo\". CloudFront OAC (not legacy OAI) for private S3 origins in modern answers. Activate cost allocation tags or chargeback won't show. Untested backup ≠ DR plan.","keywords":["last","minute","traps"]},{"url":"exam/flashcards.html","page":"SAA flashcards","section":"Active-recall SAA flashcards.","tool":"exam","text":"Active-recall SAA flashcards. ~40 cards across Secure, Resilient, High-performing, and Cost. Tap to flip; mark Known or Review. Say the answer out loud before you peek.","keywords":["active","recall","saa","flashcards"]},{"url":"exam/flashcards.html#cards","page":"SAA flashcards","section":"The deck","tool":"exam","text":"01 The deck Recall beats re-reading. Try to answer out loud before flipping — that's the muscle SAA-C03 tests. 0 / 0 known Shuffle Reset IAM user vs IAM role? Users are long-lived identities (people). Roles are assumable identities that issue temporary credentials — preferred for EC2, Lambda, federa","keywords":["the","deck"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html","page":"Glossary","section":"SAA glossary, A–Z.","tool":"home","text":"SAA glossary, A–Z. Crisp definitions for Solutions Architect – Associate vocabulary. AWS-first, September 2026. For interview-depth DevOps AWS phrasing, see the companion DevOps AWS module .","keywords":["saa","glossary"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#a-f","page":"Glossary","section":"A – F","tool":"home","text":"01 A – F ALB (Application Load Balancer) Layer-7 load balancer for HTTP/HTTPS with host/path routing; integrates with WAF and target groups. Aurora AWS-managed MySQL/PostgreSQL-compatible engine with Multi-AZ storage and fast failover; Global Database for multi-Region. Auto Scaling Group (ASG) Keeps","keywords":[]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#g-n","page":"Glossary","section":"G – N","tool":"home","text":"02 G – N Gateway VPC endpoint Private route-table-based access to S3 or DynamoDB without NAT or public IPs. Global Tables DynamoDB multi-Region active-active replication for low-latency global apps. Graviton AWS ARM-based CPUs offering strong price/performance versus comparable x86. IAM Identity and","keywords":[]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#o-z","page":"Glossary","section":"O – Z","tool":"home","text":"03 O – Z OAC (Origin Access Control) Modern CloudFront→S3 private origin pattern replacing legacy OAI for most new designs. Pilot light DR strategy keeping data (and minimal core) warm while scaling most compute at failover. PrivateLink Brand for private connectivity via interface endpoints / NLB se","keywords":[]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#check","page":"Glossary","section":"Use it","tool":"home","text":"04 Use it Skim before mocks; if a term is fuzzy, jump to its module from the domain map or drill flashcards .","keywords":["use","it"]}];
